#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097832 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2\\mathbf{a} &= 2 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1.5\\mathbf{a} &= 1.5 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a$, and are parallel to each other." :tutorial/id #uuid "977eec28-841a-556b-a6c4-937a625db1b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097834 :tutorial/content "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector:\n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5934891-d63d-5d05-be3e-b864a8081187" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2907 :tutorial/title "The Zero Vector is Parallel to Any Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097852 :tutorial/content "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation\n$\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}$\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "9fc7b4c2-c418-5980-88be-ea7609a45ba0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3401 :tutorial/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors Are Parallel"}]}
